I have a suggestion for the question of how spoily the Guidebook should 
be. Back when I played adventure games there was a program called the 
Universal Hint System (it probably still exists), for which you could 
download a data file for most popular adventure games. Basically, if you 
were stuck you could click on a general question and get a vague, 
general answer, and if that didn't help you or you were just feeling 
lazy you could click on a more specific question and get a more specific 
answer and so on down to the point where it took your hand and stepped 
you through. Basically you could do a similar thing with the Guidebook 
using HTML.

First problem: the Guidebook would no longer be plain text. On the other 
hand restricting it to simple markup would allow it to be browsed with 
Lynx so shell users would be allowed for.

Second problem: the Guidebook would no longer be a single file. It would 
need to be provided for download as a zip and as a compressed tar, and 
of course it could also be hosted on web servers.

If there is any interest in this idea I would be happy to do a first 
draft during the week leading up to Christmas (I'm stuck in the sticks 
with an underpowered laptop and I can't spend the whole time playing 
Nethack) and put it up on my web server for people to look at.


